// S7-200 SMART STL template for the ATEQ four-bit handshake.
//
// Before use in STEP 7-MicroWIN SMART:
// 1. Map Start_Request and Reset_Request to the confirmed physical/source signals.
// 2. T37/T38 are 100 ms TON timers on S7-200 SMART; PT=20 means 2.0 seconds.
// 3. M10.0/M10.1 are internal pulse states; confirm that they and T37/T38 are free.
// 4. PC owns M0.2/M0.3. Only the two refresh networks below may write M0.0/M0.1.
//
// Symbol table (addresses must be confirmed on site):
// Start_Request   Start request, normally open
// Reset_Request   Reset request, normally open

// First scan: clear internal states and the complete handshake byte.
NETWORK 1
LD     SM0.1
R      M10.0, 2
R      M0.0, 4

// Keep internal pulse states separate from the shared MB0. The PC performs a
// read-modify-write on MB0; these refresh networks restore PLC-owned bits each scan.
NETWORK 2
LD     Start_Request
EU
S      M10.0, 1

NETWORK 3
LD     M10.0
TON    T37, 20

NETWORK 4
LD     T37
R      M10.0, 1

NETWORK 5
LD     M10.0
=      M0.0

// A held Reset_Request does not retrigger; it must return OFF first.
NETWORK 6
LD     Reset_Request
EU
S      M10.1, 1

NETWORK 7
LD     M10.1
TON    T38, 20

NETWORK 8
LD     T38
R      M10.1, 1

NETWORK 9
LD     M10.1
=      M0.1

// PC -> PLC result signals (no PLC-side timer or reset logic here):
// M0.2 = PASS pulse, written and cleared by the PC after 2 seconds.
// M0.3 = FAIL pulse, written and cleared by the PC after 2 seconds.
// Use M0.2/M0.3 rising edges in the PLC sequence as required by the machine.
